# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, Git on Windows checks source out with CRLF (core.autocrlf
# defaults to true there), while .rustfmt.toml pins `newline_style = "Unix"`.
# `cargo fmt --check` then fails on every file on a Windows runner even though
# the repository content is correct. Pinning LF makes every checkout identical
# on every platform.
#
# LF also matters for the shell scripts: a CRLF `#!/usr/bin/env bash` line makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter named "bash\r", which fails with a
# confusing "bad interpreter" error.

# Normalize everything text-like to LF in the index and on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the file types that must not vary.
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
*.desktop   text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.rb        text eol=lf

# PowerShell and Inno Setup are Windows-only, but LF keeps the tree uniform and
# both tools read it correctly. The .iss additionally carries a UTF-8 BOM, which
# Git preserves because the file is still detected as text.
*.ps1       text eol=lf
*.iss       text eol=lf

# Never touched: normalization would corrupt these.
*.ico       binary
*.icns      binary
*.ttf       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gz        binary
*.zip       binary
*.dmg       binary
*.pkg       binary
*.deb       binary
*.exe       binary

# Keep generated and vendored files out of language statistics and diffs.
Cargo.lock  linguist-generated=true -diff
